markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Features as a Python Dictionary

Before your students write any Python, they need to know what the numbers in the code mean.

A note can be described by three features.
- **Frequency** is how high or low the sound is, measured in hertz (Hz), or vibrations per second. A higher frequency sounds higher in pitch.
-  **Volume** is how loud the sound is, measured in decibels (dB).
-   **Brightness** is how sharp or bright the tone sounds, which depends on how much of the sound is made of higher frequencies.

Here's how the slides describe a cello note and a violin note:

<div style="display: flex; gap: 20px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 260px;">
    <div style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 8px;">Cello</div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px;">
      <div style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; color: #222222;">Frequency</div>
      <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 18px; font-size: 18px; color: #222222;">300 Hz</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 12px 16px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px;">
      <div style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; color: #222222;">Volume</div>
      <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 18px; font-size: 18px; color: #222222;">60 dB</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-top: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px;">
      <div style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; color: #222222;">Brightness</div>
      <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 18px; font-size: 18px; color: #222222;">20</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 260px;">
    <div style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 8px;">Violin</div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px;">
      <div style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; color: #222222;">Frequency</div>
      <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 18px; font-size: 18px; color: #222222;">300 Hz</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 12px 16px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px;">
      <div style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; color: #222222;">Volume</div>
      <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 18px; font-size: 18px; color: #222222;">60 dB</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-top: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px;">
      <div style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; color: #222222;">Brightness</div>
      <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 18px; font-size: 18px; color: #222222;">35</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

These two notes share the same frequency and volume, but the violin is brighter. That matters in the lab, because a classifier makes its decision by asking whether a feature is **greater than** or **less than** a number. Without knowing what 300 Hz or 60 dB describes, a line like "frequency greater than 400" is just a number with no meaning.

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <p>&#128161; <strong>Teacher tip:</strong> students often mix up frequency and volume. Frequency is pitch, how high or low a sound is. Volume is loudness. A quiet sound can have a high frequency, and a loud sound can have a low one.</p>
</div>

## From a Note to a Prediction

To describe a note with more than one feature at once, Python uses a dictionary: a collection of named values bundled under one variable. Keys, like "frequency" and "volume", name each feature. Values, like 300 and 60, hold each feature's number.

A dictionary only holds information, so it doesn't predict anything by itself. That's what a conditional does, the same tool from Lesson 6's thermostat, just checking a feature instead of a temperature. Step through all three ideas below. Switch between the cello and the violin to see how the same code treats each one.

<div style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden; margin:20px 0;">
  <iframe
    src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/AIFY2/unit-2/note-classifier-walkthrough.html"
    width="100%"
    height="780"
    frameborder="0"
    scrolling="no"
    title="Step through a Python dictionary, a conditional, and a nested conditional that classify a cello or violin note">
  </iframe>
</div>

The number a conditional compares against is called a cutoff. Notice that frequency alone can't separate these two notes, because they share the same frequency, which is why the lab asks students to nest a conditional and check brightness too.

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <p>&#128161; <strong>Teacher tip:</strong> frequency, volume, and brightness are features, the same term from Lesson 8's classifiers. This lesson doesn't redefine it, but it's worth reminding students that the vocabulary carries over: a feature is still just a piece of information used to make a prediction.</p>
</div>

In the next level, students will use features read straight out of a dictionary like this one to write their first classifier that tells a cello from a violin.
MARKDOWN
